a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3(-1)^{4} & 3(-1)^{2}+12(-1)(-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(-1)-1 \\\\ (-1)^{3}+3(-1)(-1)^{4}+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 15-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{12} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.083 \\\\ -0.917\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139536043 true] [17592186204448 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536043 true] [17592186204448 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - xy - 3 \\\\ x^{2} - x + y - 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{2} \\approx []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"alternative-to-newtons-method-for-multivariable-functions-using-linear-systems\"></a>" 13194139536043 false] [17592186204448 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139536043 true] [17592186204448 1145 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-x+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-x+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n