 zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139536043 true] [17592186380745 1113 #uuid "bed99fc1-5ccd-5215-9718-d009510b2e2b" 13194139536043 true] [17592186380745 1145 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(3) & -2(2) \\\\ 2(3) & 2(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}-(2)^{2}-4 \\\\ (3)^{2}+(2)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}12 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligne